1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,960
Every architect knows this horror story.

2
00:00:04,480 --> 00:00:07,504
A door moves just over
a metre on a revision.

3
00:00:07,723 --> 00:00:11,171
The drawings go out. And the
first time someone opens it…

4
00:00:11,339 --> 00:00:13,404
it slams into the furniture.

5
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:19,579
PLANRIFT catches that before
the revision is issued.

6
00:00:23,759 --> 00:00:26,104
This is a live model of a hotel room.

7
00:00:26,325 --> 00:00:29,597
Every number you see is
computed, not typed in.

8
00:00:30,280 --> 00:00:32,603
Grab the door and slide it along the wall.

9
00:00:32,829 --> 00:00:35,961
The engine re-checks the swing
against every piece of furniture,

10
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:37,843
to the millimetre, as you move.

11
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:42,921
In the studio, we start
from the approved plan.

12
00:00:43,207 --> 00:00:45,841
The door clears the console
by more than a metre,

13
00:00:45,881 --> 00:00:47,281
and the strip along the wall shows

14
00:00:47,321 --> 00:00:49,968
the result for every
position it could take.

15
00:00:55,180 --> 00:00:57,902
Revision A moves it to
three thousand millimetres.

16
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:04,054
Now the leaf hits the console after
just fifteen point seven degrees,

17
00:01:04,139 --> 00:01:07,935
and would push one hundred and eighty-eight
point seven millimetres into it.

18
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:12,568
Plan and 3D can't disagree:
both come from the same scene,

19
00:01:12,608 --> 00:01:15,675
and every status is decided
with exact integer maths.

20
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:20,052
Revision B tries twenty-four hundred.

21
00:01:20,230 --> 00:01:24,212
Full swing. Three hundred and
ninety-one point five millimetres clear.

22
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:29,657
But clear isn't finished. Three
documents show this door: the floor plan,

23
00:01:29,697 --> 00:01:32,197
the room elevation and the door schedule.

24
00:01:32,680 --> 00:01:35,700
PLANRIFT flags each one
for a person to reissue.

25
00:01:35,807 --> 00:01:39,000
It never edits them, and it
leaves the room next door alone.

26
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:43,262
Compare both revisions
with the approved plan.

27
00:01:43,426 --> 00:01:47,484
Submit for review, acknowledge
the flagged sheets, and accept.

28
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:50,823
The decision becomes a receipt, with
a fingerprint of the scene before

29
00:01:50,863 --> 00:01:52,003
and after.

30
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:54,965
Under the hood, it's a Frankenstein:

31
00:01:55,081 --> 00:02:00,390
a CAD-style editor, a geometry engine,
a live 3D room, revision control,

32
00:02:00,430 --> 00:02:04,105
a document ledger and an audit
trail, stitched into one loop.

33
00:02:04,325 --> 00:02:06,565
One edit flows through all of them.

34
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:16,521
Kiro built it with five specs, nine
steering files and seven custom agents.

35
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:22,268
Six hooks kept it honest: tests gate
every task, secrets can’t be written,

36
00:02:22,308 --> 00:02:24,440
and nothing goes public without asking.

37
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:38,275
PLANRIFT. Know what a revision
breaks, before it’s issued.
